# Normalise line endings so the dependency guards cannot be silenced by a
# CRLF checkout (#96). Dart regexes anchor before \r, which made every rule
# read an empty file on Windows.
#
# What this does NOT do, verified by constructing a CRLF blob with
# `git hash-object --no-filters` and checking it out (#118):
#
#   - It normalises on CHECK-IN of newly staged content. A blob already
#     committed with CRLF stays that way; `git add --renormalize .` is the
#     missing half, and is a one-off someone has to run.
#   - It does nothing at all for lone `\r`. That is handled in the rules
#     themselves — `normaliseText` translates rather than deletes, because
#     deleting collapsed such a file into one line and silenced every rule
#     (#112).
#
# The rules do not depend on this file being effective. It is a convenience
# for the working tree; the guard is normaliseText.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
# Hashed byte-for-byte by tools/fetch_fonts.sh; upstream's IBM Plex Mono
# licence has CRLF endings, which text=auto would rewrite on checkout (#47).
assets/fonts/OFL-*.txt -text
